WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.040
tmpfs is a memory-backed filesystem commonly used for temporary storage on Linux. Files

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:08.740
stored in tmpfs live in virtual memory.

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:13.120
Depending on system configuration, pressure, and swap behavior,

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:15.580
tmpfs pages may interact with swap,

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:21.080
but the important design idea is that tmpfs is not ordinary persistent disk storage.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:24.900
It is useful for temporary files, runtime state, caches,

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:28.160
and writable layers that do not need to survive reboot.

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:32.700
Initramfs is different but related to memory-backed booting. It is an early boot

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:35.080
environment loaded into memory with the kernel.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:39.860
The initramfs can contain tools and scripts needed before the final root filesystem is

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:40.880
ready.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:46.940
In RAM-boot designs, initramfs logic may copy images, prepare overlays, load drivers,

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:51.160
mount devices, or decide which filesystem becomes the real root.

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:54.660
RAM-backed storage competes with the rest of the system.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:01:01.040
A root image copied into RAM, a tmpfs upper layer, browser memory, desktop graphics,

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:05.500
containers, package updates, build tools, file cache,

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:08.120
and applications all share finite memory.

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:13.140
A system that boots successfully with no workload may fail under realistic use.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:16.240
A desktop with a browser, GPU stack,

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:20.380
and development tools has different pressure than a kiosk running one application.

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:23.080
Volatility is the defining tradeoff.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:29.060
If changes live only in RAM, they disappear when the system reboots, crashes, loses power,

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:31.200
or discards the tmpfs layer.

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:34.220
That can be excellent for clean labs and kiosks.

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:40.440
It can also lose logs, package updates, SSH host keys, machine identity, browser profiles,

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:43.700
and user documents if persistence was not designed.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:48.000
Before committing to any design, measure. Watch memory use after boot,

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:51.740
under real workload, during updates, and during shutdown.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:56.600
Consider whether swap is present and whether swap changes the privacy or performance

00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:59.940
expectations. Test low-memory behavior.

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:05.840
Document what is volatile, what is persistent, and what happens when RAM pressure rises.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:10.140
A RAM-backed system is only reliable if its memory budget matches the workload.
